WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.198 --> 00:00:01.740
ALICIA WILLIAMS:
Today, we're talking

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:06.900
about BigQuery, VMware Engine,
ML, and carbon emissions.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.110
I'm Alicia, and welcome to "This
Week in Cloud," where we serve

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:13.320
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.800
Last year, we announced our most
ambitious sustainability goal

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:20.490
yet, to operate
everywhere on 24/7 carbon

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:22.950
free energy by 2030.

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:25.680
Since setting our target,
we've made tremendous progress

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.860
in how we track, buy, and
use electricity, advocate

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.380
for clean energy
policies, and support

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.210
the development of
new technologies

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.140
to help us reach this goal.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.020
And in the spirit
of transparency,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:38.850
we've released some
further information

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.310
about our 2020 consumption.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.320
The "A Year in
Carbon Free Energy"

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.960
animation points out
significant projects

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.650
that came online in 2020 to
bring our data centers closer

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.800
to operating entirely on
round-the-clock carbon

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.060
free energy.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.040
Check it out on the blog.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.250
With all the updates from
Google Cloud VMware Engine,

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.940
we've devoted a whole blog post
to breaking them down for you.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.430
As a teaser, Google
Cloud VMware Engine

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:08.820
delivers an enterprise-grade
VMware stack running natively

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.440
in Google Cloud.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.900
This Cloud service is
one of the fastest paths

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.910
to the Cloud for
VMware workloads,

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:17.370
without making changes
to existing applications

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.210
or operating models across
a variety of use cases.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.270
Head over to the
blog post for more.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.910
The latest round of ML
perf benchmark results

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.630
have been released, and
Google's TPU v4 supercomputers

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:34.620
demonstrated record-breaking
performance at scale.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:37.440
These scores are a significant
improvement over our winning

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.720
submission from last
year, and demonstrate

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.690
that Google once again has
the world's fastest Machine

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.730
Learning supercomputers.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:48.360
These TPU v4 pods are
already widely deployed

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.000
throughout Google data centers
for our internal Machine

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.030
Learning workloads, and will
be available via Google Cloud

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.120
later this year.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.270
If you haven't yet, check out
our new BigQuery spotlight

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.280
series, where
throughout the summer,

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.950
we'll be adding new videos
and blog posts focused

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:07.010
on helping new BigQuery
architects and administrators

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.660
master the fundamentals.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.840
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:14.550
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.070
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:18.210
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.830
We'll see you in the Cloud.

